12:["$","article",null,{"children":[["$","$L1c",null,{"topic":{"id":"trauma-and-ptsd","title":"Trauma & PTSD: When the Past Keeps Arriving in the Present","subtitle":"Flashbacks, hypervigilance, and numbing are protection, not malfunction.","category":"Trauma & PTSD","tone":"lavender","icon":"shieldAlert","keywords":["trauma","PTSD","flashback","hypervigilance","triggers","EMDR"],"readingTime":9,"featuredImage":"/ff47f82f-c7ed-4863-b7ca-fd3bac014a4a.jpg","excerpt":"Trauma is stored differently from ordinary memory, which is why it returns as sensation rather than story. Grounding and trauma-focused therapy both work with that.","reviewer":"Reviewed by Dr. Sarah Jenkins, PsyD","updated":"Updated August 2026","intro":["A traumatic memory is not filed the way other memories are. Under extreme threat, the brain prioritises survival over narrative, so fragments — a smell, a tone of voice, a body position — get stored without a timestamp.","When a fragment is triggered later, the body responds as if the event is happening now. That is what makes flashbacks so disorienting: the experience is real, but the clock is wrong."],"mechanics":{"heading":"Threat memory without a timestamp","body":["Hypervigilance, startle responses, and numbing are all downstream of a threat-detection system that has raised its baseline. Nothing is broken; the setting is simply stuck on high after it was genuinely needed.","Grounding works by giving the brain unambiguous present-tense evidence — cold water, textured objects, naming the year out loud. Trauma-focused therapy then helps the memory be re-filed with its correct date attached."]},"symptoms":[{"label":"Intrusive re-experiencing","description":"Images, sounds, or bodily sensations that arrive uninvited and feel current.","kind":"Mind"},{"label":"Startle and scanning","description":"Sitting facing exits, jumping at doors, exhausting alertness in safe rooms.","kind":"Body"},{"label":"Emotional numbing","description":"Feeling distant from people and from yourself, as if behind glass.","kind":"Mind"},{"label":"Trigger avoidance","description":"Routing around places, media, or conversations, gradually shrinking daily life.","kind":"Behavior"}],"myths":[{"myth":"Only combat veterans get PTSD.","fact":"Assault, accidents, medical events, childbirth, childhood neglect, and sustained abuse can all cause PTSD."},{"myth":"Talking about it always makes it worse.","fact":"Unstructured retelling can overwhelm, but structured trauma-focused therapy is what most reliably reduces symptoms."},{"myth":"If it happened long ago, it should be over.","fact":"Untreated trauma has no expiry date. Symptoms can surface decades later, often after a period of apparent stability."}],"strategies":[{"name":"Orienting to the present","summary":"Reasserts the correct date and place when a flashback begins.","steps":["Say the year, month, and your age out loud.","Name five objects in the room and one thing you can hear.","Press your feet into the floor and feel the chair behind you.","Add temperature — cold water on the wrists or a cold glass in hand."]},{"name":"Window-of-tolerance check","summary":"Names your current state so you can choose the right tool.","steps":["Rate arousal now: shut down, settled, or over-activated.","If over-activated, use long exhales and cold sensation.","If shut down, use gentle movement, standing, or a bilateral tap."]},{"name":"Safety-anchor kit","summary":"Puts grounding within reach before it is needed.","steps":["Assemble a strong scent, a textured object, and headphones.","Add a card with three phrases that reliably help you.","Keep one kit at home and a small version in a bag."]}],"treatments":[{"name":"Eye Movement Desensitisation & Reprocessing","abbreviation":"EMDR","description":"Uses bilateral stimulation while recalling the memory so it can be reprocessed with far less physiological charge."},{"name":"Trauma-Focused CBT","abbreviation":"TF-CBT","description":"Combines gradual, controlled exposure with cognitive work on guilt, blame, and safety beliefs."},{"name":"Somatic and body-based therapy","abbreviation":"SE","description":"Works with the nervous system directly — useful when talking triggers overwhelm or memory is fragmentary."}],"support":{"guidance":"Predictability is the whole job. Announce changes, avoid surprise touch, and let them keep control of doors, exits, and pace. You are not there to retrieve details.","say":["“You are safe here. It is 2026 and you are in your kitchen.”","“Do you want me closer or a little further away?”","“You never have to tell me what happened.”"],"avoid":["“What exactly happened to you?”","“You need to move on from this.”","“It couldn’t have been that bad.”"]},"faqs":[{"question":"Can PTSD be treated successfully?","answer":"Yes. EMDR and trauma-focused CBT produce substantial improvement for most people, often within 8–20 sessions."},{"question":"What if I cannot remember it clearly?","answer":"Fragmentary memory is typical and does not disqualify you from treatment. Therapies work with the fragments and the body’s response."},{"question":"Is it normal to feel nothing at all?","answer":"Yes. Numbing is one of the recognised symptom clusters, and it often lifts as safety is re-established."}],"pacer":[{"label":"Gentle 4-4-6","inhale":4,"hold":4,"exhale":6,"description":"Short holds. Long breath-holding can feel unsafe when trauma is active."},{"label":"Even 5-0-5","inhale":5,"hold":0,"exhale":5,"description":"No hold at all — continuous, predictable rhythm for grounding."}]}}],"$L1d","$L1e"]}]
